  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nSoutheast sfc flow and shortwave energy aloft continue to stream \nlow to mid level moisture into the region. This is resulting in some \ndrizzle under the lower clouds. Other than increasing winds along \nand west of the Missouri this afternoon, there's nothing that will \nreally change this pattern today. Tonight, a llj develops and \nheights aloft begin to rise. Gusty winds spread farther east on \nWednesday, and the moisture gets a bump with the llj increasing to \n40 kts. Showers and embedded weak thunderstorms will develop with \nthe llj, enhanced on Wednesday and pushing east with a shortwave \ntrough and weak low pressure along the Rockies. Wednesday into \nWednesday night will see the most widespread light rainfall of the \nweek. Generally, however, highest amounts of a quarter to half an \ninch will be confined to areas east of the James Valley. \n\nA drier air mass pushes in behind this system, and by Thursday \nafternoon areas west of the James Valley may see some clearing. \nRidging aloft will help push temps up into the 70s with any \nsunshine. On Friday, though, another shortwave trough moves north \nthrough South Dakota and light rain and clouds return.",
